1. The validation of the proposed controller is still limited. This study mainly relies on simulations
and cyclic loop experiments, but this may not fully represent the individualized cardiovascular
dynamics of real patients, different ventricular assist devices (VADs), long-term physiological
changes, or complex pathological conditions. The authors should clearly distinguish which
results come from simulations and which come from physical simulation loops, and further
validate the method with more diverse preload/postload conditions, severity of heart failure, and
ideally using in vivo or clinical data in ideal scenarios.

2. Some key method details are insufficiently described, which limits the reproducibility of the results. The paper should provide precise reward weights, target values for mean arterial
pressure (MAP), suction threshold, reflux threshold, allowed pump speed range, maximum
speed change limit, peak power output (PPO) hyperparameters, number of training sets, random
seed, convergence criteria, and implementation details of offline pre-training. Since the results
of reinforcement learning are highly sensitive to these parameters, it is recommended to provide
a complete parameter table or pseudo-code.

3. Statistical analysis needs to be strengthened. Although the paper reports the average values and
0.95 confidence intervals of MAP RMSE, flow rate RMSE, unsafe rate, recovery time and other
indicators, it does not clearly state the number of repeated experiments or random seeds used to
calculate these intervals. Moreover, there is a lack of formal statistical comparisons between the
proposed method and the baseline controller. Appropriate statistical tests and multiple
comparison corrections should be added to support the claimed performance improvement.

4. The baseline comparison is not comprehensive enough. The current comparisons mainly include
fixed-speed control and the weakened versions of the proposed model, such as MLP PPO, only
CNN PPO, only Transformer PPO, and the reward ablation baseline. These are useful for
ablation analysis, but they are not sufficient to prove that it is superior to the existing VAD
control strategies. The authors should include stronger traditional baselines and learning-based
baselines under the same experimental settings, such as PI control, fuzzy control, Starling-like
control, model predictive control, DDPG, TD3, or previously reported DRL-based VAD
controllers.

5. The safety and clinical applicability of the proposed method require further in-depth discussion.
The definition of "unacceptable rate" should be clarified, including whether it refers to
inhalation, reflux, backflow, excessive fluctuation in speed, hemolysis risk, or other
hemodynamic instability. Additionally, the controller uses left ventricular pressure (LVP), mean
arterial pressure (MAP), pump flow rate, and previous pump speed as input signals, but LVP
and MAP may require invasive monitoring, and they may not be continuously available in
actual VAD patients. The authors should discuss how to obtain these signals in the actual
system, whether sensor-free estimation is needed, and how sensor noise, signal delay, signal
loss, or measurement drift will affect the safety of the controller.